  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn upper level high remains centered over Arizona and New Mexico\ntoday, bringing well above normal temperatures across the\nregion. Available moisture today is rather lacking with the\nplacement of the upper high, though lingering moisture has\nallowed for a weak thunderstorms to develop over mainly high \nelevation locations of southeastern Arizona this afternoon. \nLarge surface dew point depressions will allow for a strong wind\ngust threat with any thunderstorm that does form, but coverage \nshould remain very isolated. \n\nThe upper high remains in a similar location on Thursday, but a\nmodest increase in gulf moisture should occur, increasing \nthunderstorm chances to mainly 10-20 percent, with 15-30 \npercent chances from south central Pima through Santa Cruz and \ninto southern Cochise. It's in these southern areas where the \n12Z HREF highlights a strong gusty wind threat with an isolated \npotential for a 50 knot gust. \n\nThe upper level pattern will undergo a transition Friday \nthrough the weekend with the center of the upper high shifting \neast through New Mexico and into Texas, while troughing sets up \nover the west coast and Great Basin region. An increase in \nprecipitable water is expected with this transition as PWAT \nvalues will likely become 110 to near 150 percent above normal \nby the end of the weekend. The combination of the support from \nthe upper level trough and increased moisture will bring \nwidespread thunderstorm chances Friday into Monday (mainly 30-60\npercent each day). Picking out which days will be more active \nor impactful than the others can be a difficult task several \ndays out, but there looks to be a bit of a better overlap in \nupper level support and deeper moisture Sunday into Monday which\ncould point to the more active period. Thunderstorm chances \nshould decrease at least somewhat going into the middle of next \nweek, but there remains model discrepancies in how to handle \nthis period. \n\nAn Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect through Saturday, with\nthe last of the peak of the heat likely occurring on Friday.\nMorning low temperatures will remain warm through this period\nwith lows over 80 degrees from Tucson westward through Saturday\nmorning.",
